00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
with every passing year it seems like  more and more physical retailers fall  off the map as they struggle to keep up  with the online revolution  retailers as big as jc penney j crew  neiman marcus tier one imports toys r us  price electronics and even sears have  all filed for bankruptcy or shut down it  completely  and with the surge of the pandemic it  seems like this trend would only  accelerate and that's exactly what  happened throughout 2020 as dyn  retailers completely collapsed  but as these weak retailers left the  space the surviving retailers have  emerged stronger than ever before  in fact according to the us government  in 2021 retail sales grew by 18 while  online sales only grew by 14  now you could chalk this up to a rebound  from pandemic lows and i'm sure that  plays a role  but this confirms that there are certain  products that people prefer to buy in  person even if they're available online  otherwise why would consumers have gone  back to physical retailers  also given that a bunch of physical  retailers shut down it during the  pandemic you would think that it would  be harder for the remaining retailers to  make a comeback but that doesn't seem to  be the case  in fact the national retail federation  predicts that 72 percent of u.s sales  will still occur in brick and mortar  stores in 2024.  so why are physical retailers making a  comeback and is this sustainable or just  a fluke  one of the biggest strengths of physical  retailers is the delivery and return  process  while amazon has continuously pushed on  delivery times from weeks to days to  hours there's often still quite a bit of  friction within these processes  first of all you have the package missed  delivery problem  i'm sure all of us have received one of  our neighbor's packages or our packages  have ended up at our neighbor's house  and sometimes you don't even know where  your package went and it's just gone  forever whenever this happens you have  to file a complaint with the retailer  and ups or fedex and it's just a massive  headache  in fact i bet a lot of consumers don't  even file lost package complaints  especially for cheap products just to  avoid the hassle  while this is simply a minor annoyance  for cheap products where this becomes a  serious problem is expensive products  i don't think any of us want to deal  with a new smartphone or laptop being  delivered to some random dude in the  city  now of course there is the option of  requesting a signed delivery for  expensive products but this isn't  necessarily all that convenient you now  have to spend your entire day at home  waiting for the delivery guy there's  also the problem of porch pirates which  is especially a problem within big  cities  in fact over 10 percent of all packages  disappear without an explanation in the  major u.s cities  and given that most of us make at least  one online purchase every month we're  talking about dealing with at least one  lost package every year and then there's  the problem of returns returning  products has always been one of the  biggest hassles of the online shopping  experience  26 percent of online shoppers say that  they never return an online order and 65  say that they rarely return an online  order  this is quite ironic when you consider  that online return rates are still way  higher than brick and mortar return  rates  on average shoppers return five to ten  percent of in-store purchases while  shoppers return 15 to 40 percent of  online purchases  this means that if most consumers  weren't too lazy to return online orders  this number would be even higher  meanwhile the successful physical  retailers have figured out how to  perfectly combine the online and  physical shopping experiences to offer  the best of both worlds  for example with most stores you can do  curbside pickup  this is perfect if you want to order  something online but don't want to deal  with the delivery with the curbside you  don't even have to get out of your car  but you can easily ensure that your  package doesn't go to someone else and  that it's the right product  also it's not really a hassle to pick up  the product either if you plan it right  you can just pick up your purchase when  you come back from work similarly if you  don't want the product you can simply  drop it off and store the next day after  work  considering this i believe a lot of  americans will prefer curbside pickup  even over home delivery  and as the convenience disparity between  physical and online shopping shrinks  physical retailers will simply continue  to make up ground  aside from physical retailers shining in  the delivery and return categories  physical retailers will continue to kill  it when it comes to on-demand products  like groceries  while there are services like instacart  and whole foods home delivery there's no  doubt that these options are still  extremely expensive  and most americans are unwilling to pay  such high delivery fees to simply  receive everyday goods like groceries  this isn't anything new but it wasn't  until recently that physical retailers  have started to leverage this advantage  specifically with curbside grocery  delivery  this isn't as advantageous for retailers  that only sell groceries but this is  extremely helpful for mega retailers  like walmart target sam's club and  costco who sell everything under the sun  let's say you need a new mouse instead  of ordering it from amazon why not  bundle it with a walmart grocery order  that you're gonna pick up later that  afternoon  not only would you receive the product  earlier but you didn't even have to do  anything extra  after all you would have gone to walmart  to pick up your groceries whether or not  you ordered the mouse  another category that won't be going  online anytime soon is home improvement  items  if you're installing a cabinet and run  out of screws it makes a lot more sense  to simply make a quick trip to home  depot or lowe's instead of trying to  order screws from amazon  also when you go to the store in person  you can usually consult with home depot  employees who are generally quite  knowledgeable home depot literally hires  professional plumbers carpenters and  electricians to help customers find  products and give them tips on  installation this has become an  invaluable service for many customers  and if you don't believe me just take a  look at home depot stock while they have  cooled down recently their stock has  been going parabolic for years  and as physical retailers continue to  embrace such on-demand categories we'll  likely see consumers spend more money at  these retailers than ever  before on top of struggling with some  inherent shortfalls online retailers  have also been losing some of the key  advantages that made them better in the  first place like price about 10 years  ago a phenomenon called showrooming was  extremely common this is when consumers  go to a store to check out a product and  then go home to purchase a product  online because it's cheaper well this  made a lot of sense a decade ago and  it's not as applicable today given that  physical retailers have managed to close  the price gap  some retailers even offer special  in-store discounts which simply further  incentivizes in-store shopping and this  makes sense given that home delivery is  not cheap by any means  amazon had to run a money losing  operation for decades in order to  eventually achieve the economies of  skill required to make home delivery  profitable and with time amazon has only  increased the cost of their prime  subscription which now sits at 140  aside from charging consumers for  delivery amazon also charges sellers for  fulfillment as well amazon sellers  generally have to pay three to six  dollars for each item amazon fulfills  depending on its size  and as you would guess sellers are just  going to pass on this cost to consumers  now you can't really blame amazon for  these fees given that their net margin  is in the single digits however this  gives physical retailers the perfect  opportunity to be competitive in terms  of price and that's exactly what they've  done while mark has actually overtaken  amazon in terms of cheapness in several  categories including baby supplies  cleaning supplies medicine and personal  hygiene in other categories such as  makeup toys and pet food amazon has  maintained a lead meanwhile in some  other categories such as snack and food  prices walmart and amazon offer  identical prices to the penny  so clearly it's no longer a case of it's  always cheaper online  now it depends on product category and  in the long term you're not really going  to save money by choosing one or the  other in terms of product prices however  by shopping at physical retailers you  don't have to pay annual subscription  fee or delivery fees so it could  actually be cheaper to shop in person  and finally as for the last main factor  driving forward to physical retailers is  the shopping experience itself nowadays  people have gotten lazier than ever  before and have arguably gotten spoiled  with home delivery but even despite this  there's something special about  in-person shopping that you just can't  get with online shopping and i'm not  talking about weekly grocery shopping or  essential purchases rather i'm talking  about purchases that you've been saving  up for and are looking forward to take  the iphone for example eight years ago i  remember watching ijustine camping  outside of an apple store to be one of  the first people in the world to get an  iphone 6. with the rise of online  shopping apple has made it possible to  simply pre-order the iphone and get it  delivered to your house on the day it's  released but having the phone delivered  to your doorstep simply doesn't have the  same excitement and satisfaction as  buying it in store  now that's of course an extreme example  but this applies to smaller purchases as  well  buying shoes online isn't the same as  going to a nike store with your friends  and i believe most consumers feel the  same way given that the majority of  shoppers still prefer to shop in store  and here's the reasons they cite for  this preference 33 of respondents to the  survey suggest that they prefer to view  touch and interact with physical  products 26 percent of respondents  suggest that they enjoy the overall  shopping experience that a physical  location provides and 13 suggest that  they like the immediacy that in-store  shopping offers hearing this physical  retailers have placed a significant  emphasis on improving the in-store  experience from buffing up customer  support to making the entire process  seamless and it seems like these  improvements have been working and given  that consumers still prefer in-store  shopping in general  throughout this entire video we've  discussed the various advantages of  physical retailers and the bigger  shortfalls of online retailers and while  online retailers do have their  disadvantages i don't want to give off  the impression that online retailers are  doing poorly people are spending more  money than ever before online and this  will only increase moving forward but  with that being said i also don't think  that innovative retailers are going  anywhere either  in fact i think they'll perform  tremendously as well  the problem with physical retailers 10  years ago was that they were just simply  trying to replicate amazon they simply  threw together a website and started  shipping products to people's houses  this wasn't very successful given that  physical retailers weren't offering  anything unique or special in comparison  to amazon  but over the years the best retailers  have learned to leverage their physical  locations to offer something that amazon  can't this includes pickup and return  convenience immediate availability and a  unique shopping experience  and as long as physical retailers  continue to lean into the factories that  make them unique i don't think they'll  be going anywhere but that's just what i  think do you guys prefer online shopping  or in-store shopping comment that down  below also drop a like if you wish your  packages didn't get lost in the mail on  a regular basis  and of course consider checking out our  international channels to watch our  videos in other languages and consider  subscribing and see more questions  logically answered but until then i'm  hari and i'll see you guys on the next  one

